
Module V: Read a Production Log — File Handling
1. Introduction to File Handling
Ab tak humare programs data ko mostly manually input lete the aur result screen par print karte the. Program band hone ke baad woh data generally save nahi hota tha.

File handling ki help se program data ko permanently store, read, update aur append kar sakta hai.

What I learned
A program jo data save nahi kar sakta, woh sirf demonstration jaisa hai. A useful production program ko records ko store aur retrieve karna aana chahiye.


2. Persistence
Persistence ka meaning hai data ka program ke stop hone ke baad bhi available rehna.

Example:

Program Running

      ↓

Data Created

      ↓

Data Saved to File

      ↓

Program Stops

      ↓

Data Still Exists


Production environment mein logs, measurements, part numbers aur inspection results ko save karna important hota hai.


3. Opening a File with open()
Python mein file open karne ke liye open() function use hota hai.

Example:

f = open("production_log.txt")


By default, file read mode ('r') mein open hoti hai.

Lekin file ko manually open karne ke bajay with open() use karna better practice hai.


4. The with open() Block
Example:

with open("production_log.txt") as f:

    for line in f:

        print(line)


with block ke andar file use hoti hai.

Jab block complete ho jaata hai, Python file ko automatically close kar deta hai.

Agar block ke andar exception bhi aa jaye, tab bhi file properly close ho jaati hai.

What I learned
with file handling ko safer banata hai because humein manually close() call karne ki zarurat nahi padti.


5. Why with is Better Than Bare open()
Without with:

f = open("production_log.txt")


# work with file


f.close()


Agar program f.close() tak pahunchne se pehle crash ho gaya, file properly close na hone ka risk ho sakta hai.

With with:

with open("production_log.txt") as f:

    # work with file


Python block ke end par file ko automatically close karta hai.

Important Rule
with block ke bahar file automatically closed hoti hai.


6. Reading a File Line by Line
Large files ke liye file ko ek-ek line read karna best approach hai.

Example:

with open("production_log.txt") as f:

    for line in f:

        print(line)


Iska advantage ye hai ki Python poori file ko ek saath memory mein load nahi karta.

Even a very large log file can be processed line by line.


7. .strip() and Skipping Blank Lines
Production logs mein extra spaces aur blank lines ho sakti hain.

Example:

with open("production_log.txt") as f:

    for line in f:

        line = line.strip()


        if not line:

            continue


        print(line)


.strip():

Beginning aur end ki whitespace remove karta hai.


if not line:

Empty lines ko identify karta hai.


continue:

Blank line ko skip karke next line par move karta hai.



8. Reading a Production Log
Example log:

PE-0137;25.02;Night

PE-0138;25.01;Day

PE-0139;25.05;Night


Hum ise Python mein read kar sakte hain:

with open("production_log.txt") as f:

    for line in f:

        line = line.strip()


        if not line:

            continue


        code, dia, shift = line.split(";")


        print(code.strip(), float(dia))


Yahan:

line.strip() → extra whitespace remove karta hai.


line.split(";") → line ko fields mein divide karta hai.


float(dia) → diameter ko number mein convert karta hai.



9. Three Ways to Read a File
Python mein file read karne ke different methods hain.

1. Line by line
for line in f:

    print(line)


Best choice for large files.

2. readlines()
lines = f.readlines()


Ye file ki saari lines ko ek list mein load karta hai.

Small files ke liye useful hai.

3. read()
text = f.read()


Ye complete file ko ek single string mein read karta hai.

Small files ke liye suitable hai.


10. The Four File Modes
Python mein commonly four important file modes hain:

Mode

Effect on existing file

Main Use

'r'

File ko sirf read karta hai; data change nahi hota

Reading logs

'w'

Existing file ko empty/truncate karta hai

Fresh report

'a'

Existing data ko preserve karke end mein add karta hai

Logs

'x'

File already exists ho to error deta hai

New file creation


11. 'r' — Read Mode
'r' ka meaning hai read only.

Example:

with open("production_log.txt", "r") as f:

    for line in f:

        print(line)


Existing file ka data change nahi hota.

Agar file exist nahi karti, Python error deta hai.

Use
Production logs ko read karne ke liye 'r' safest mode hai.


12. 'w' — Write Mode
'w' mode dangerous ho sakta hai.

Agar file already exist karti hai, to Python us file ka existing content immediately erase/truncate kar deta hai when the file is opened.

Example:

with open("production_log.txt", "w") as f:

    f.write("New report")


Agar pehle file mein important records the, woh erase ho sakte hain.

Important Warning
'w' data ko write karne ke time nahi, file open karte hi truncate karta hai.

Isliye existing log ko accidentally 'w' mode mein open nahi karna chahiye.


13. 'a' — Append Mode
'a' mode existing data ko delete nahi karta.

Ye new data ko file ke end mein add karta hai.

Example:

with open("production_log.txt", "a") as f:

    f.write("PE-0140;25.03;Night\n")


Existing records safe rahenge aur new record end mein add ho jayega.

Use
Production logs mein new records continuously add karne ke liye 'a' mode useful hai.

Important Rule
Log ko add karna hai → 'a'

Purani file replace karni hai → 'w'


14. 'x' — Exclusive Creation Mode
'x' mode ka use ek new file create karne ke liye hota hai.

Agar file already exist karti hai, Python error dega.

Example:

with open("new_log.txt", "x") as f:

    f.write("First record\n")


Agar new_log.txt already exist karti hai, file overwrite nahi hogi.

What I learned
'x' mode useful hai jab humein ensure karna ho ki existing file accidentally overwrite na ho.


15. What We Learned + Main Idea
From this topic, I learned:

File handling allows programs to store data permanently.


Persistence means data remains available after the program stops.


open() is used to open a file.


with open() automatically closes the file when the block ends.


Files can be processed efficiently line by line.


.strip() removes unwanted whitespace.


continue can be used to skip blank lines.


read() reads the complete file as one string.


readlines() reads all lines into a list.


for line in f reads the file one line at a time.


'r' is used for reading.


'w' overwrites/truncates an existing file immediately when opened.


'a' preserves existing data and adds new data at the end.


'x' creates a new file only if it does not already exist.


We should never use 'w' on an important log unless we intentionally want to replace its contents.


Main Idea
Production Data

      ↓

    File

      ↓

with open(...)

      ↓

Read / Process / Write

      ↓

Saved Production Records


Easy Way to Remember
'r' → Read

'w' → Wipe and Write

'a' → Add at the end

'x' → Create eXclusively


Most important lesson:

Use 'r' to read, 'a' to add to a log, 'w' only when you intentionally want to replace the file, and 'x' when overwriting an existing file must be impossible.

